" and \"Mark it down\" are two\n			-- ends of one task, so the second spot is the point of writing them.\n			spots = 2,\n			seconds = 12,\n		},\n		{\n			id = \"rush\",\n			label = \"Rush the hotfix\",\n			mark = \"Patch it\",\n			handMark = \"Mark it down\",\n			spots = 2,\n			rush = 3,\n			seconds = 30,\n			customer = \"It's on fire. Please hurry.\",\n		},\n	},\n\n	home_healthcare = {\n		{\n			id = \"check_vitals\",\n			label = \"Check the patient vitals\",\n			mark = \"Record it\",\n			spots = 1,\n			seconds = 14,\n		},\n		{\n			id = \"med_reminder\",\n			label = \"Administer the medication reminder\",\n			mark = \"Log it\",\n			spots = 1,\n			seconds = 12,\n		},\n		{\n			id = \"log_notes\",\n			label = \"Write the care notes\",\n			mark = \"Note it\",\n			spots = 1,\n			seconds = 16,\n		},\n		{\n			id = \"care_plan\",\n			label = \"Update the care plan\",\n			mark = \"Draft it\",\n			spots = 1,\n			seconds = 20,\n		},\n		{\n			id = \"rush\",\n			label = \"Rush the urgent visit\",\n			mark = \"Race it\",\n			handMark = \"Mark it down\",\n			spots = 2,\n			rush = 3,\n			seconds = 30,\n			customer = \"They need help now, please!\",\n		},\n	},\n\n	house_sitter = {\n		{\n			id = \"feed_pet\",\n			label = \"Feed the pets\",\n			mark = \"Serve it\",\n			spots = 1,\n			seconds = 10,\n		},\n		{\n			id = \"water_plants\",\n			label = \"Water the plants\",\n			mark = \"Pour it\",\n			spots = 1,\n			seconds = 12,\n		},\n		{\n			id = \"collect_mail\",\n			label = \"Collect and sort the mail\",\n			mark = \"Gather it\",\n			spots = 1,\n			seconds = 14,\n		},\n		{\n			id = \"security_check\",\n			label = \"Do the security round\",\n			mark = \"Check it\",\n			spots = 1,\n			seconds = 16,\n		},\n		{\n			id = \"rush\",\n			label = \"Rush the late-night check\",\n			mark = \"Sweep it\",\n			handMark = \"Mark it down\",\n			spots = 2,\n			rush = 3,\n			seconds = 30,\n			customer = \"Something's not right. Can you come back?\",\n		},\n	},\n}\n\n-- Content mistakes are authoring bugs, not runtime conditions, so these throw at\n-- start rather than producing a shift that quietly has nothing in it. The symptom\n-- they prevent is the worst kind: not an error, just a job that is boring, which\n-- nobody would think to look in a content file for.\nlocal function validate(jobId: string, task: JobTask, seen: { [string]: true })\n	if seen[task.id] then\n		error(`[AGES content] job \"{jobId}\" has two tasks called \"{task.id}\"`)\n	end\n	seen[task.id] = true\n\n	if task.label == \"\" then\n		error(`[AGES content] task \"{jobId}/{task.id}\" has no label; it is the only thing telling the player what to do`)\n	end\n	if task.mark == \"\" then\n		error(`[AGES content] task \"{jobId}/{task.id}\" has nothing written on its mark`)\n	end\n	if task.handMark ~= nil and task.handMark == \"\" then\n		error(\n			`[AGES content] task \"{jobId}/{task.id}\" has an empty far mark; leave the field out `\n				.. `and both ends will say \"{task.mark}\"`\n		)\n	end\n	if task.handMark ~= nil and task.spots < 2 then\n		error(`[AGES content] task \"{jobId}/{task.id}\" has a far mark and only one mark to put it on`)\n	end\n	if task.spots < 1 or task.spots > MAX_SPOTS or task.spots ~= math.floor(task.spots) then\n		error(\n			`[AGES content] task \"{jobId}/{task.id}\" wants {task.spots} marks; it must be a whole number from 1 to {MAX_SPOTS}`\n		)\n	end\n	if task.customer ~= nil and task.customer == \"\" then\n		error(\n			`[AGES content] task \"{jobId}/{task.id}\" brings somebody to wait on it and gives them nothing to say; `\n				.. `leave the field out entirely if the task has nobody in it`\n		)\n	end\n	-- A rush is the only field here that changes the shape of a task rather than its\n	-- wording, and it only works on top of the other two. Both of these would otherwise\n	-- fail quietly and confusingly: a one-mark rush is served by standing still, and a\n	-- rush with nobody in it is an ordinary task run three times for no stated reason.\n	local rush = task.rush\n	if rush ~= nil then\n		if rush ~= math.floor(rush) or rush < 2 or rush > MAX_QUEUE then\n			error(\n				`[AGES content] task \"{jobId}/{task.id}\" queues {rush} people; it must be a whole number `\n					.. `from 2 to {MAX_QUEUE} -- a queue of one is just a task`\n			)\n		end\n		if task.spots ~= 2 then\n			error(\n				`[AGES content] task \"{jobId}/{task.id}\" is a rush with {task.spots} mark(s); it needs exactly 2, `\n					.. `because a rush is those two alternated once per person`\n			)\n		end\n		if task.customer == nil then\n			error(\n				`[AGES content] task \"{jobId}/{task.id}\" is a rush with nobody queueing for it; `\n					.. `the line of people is the only thing that makes it read as one`\n			)\n		end\n	end\n\n	if task.seconds < MIN_SECONDS then\n		error(\n			`[AGES content] task \"{jobId}/{task.id}\" allows {task.seconds}s, which is under the {MIN_SECONDS}s floor `\n				.. `a player needs to cross a room -- a task nobody can finish is a punishment, not a task`\n		)\n	end\nend\n\nfor jobId, tasks in SOURCE do\n	if #tasks == 0 then\n		error(`[AGES content] job \"{jobId}\" has an empty task list; leave it out entirely instead`)\n	end\n	local seen: { [string]: true } = {}\n	for _, task in tasks do\n		validate(jobId, task, seen)\n	end\nend\n\n-- Every task a job has, or an empty list. Empty is a legitimate answer and not an\n-- error: a job with no tasks authored yet still pays, still counts hours and still\n-- climbs the ladder -- it simply has nothing to do in it, which is exactly where\n-- every job in this game was before this file existed.\nfunction JobTasks.For(jobId: string): { JobTask }\n	return SOURCE[jobId] or {}\nend\n\nreturn JobTasks\n"
table.insert(AGES_MANIFEST, "src/server/content/JobTasks.luau")
AGES_SOURCES["src/server/content/Jobs.luau"] = "--!strict\n-- The jobs that exist. Two, and they are the two ends of the building we already\n-- put on the street: the shop floor is on the ground and the office is up the\n-- stairs, so the career ladder is a literal staircase the player has to be old\n-- enough to climb.\n--\n-- Salaries are authored yearly and only yearly. What a shift pays and what an\n-- hour pays are divided out of this at the point of use, against\n-- Config.Work.YearGameHours, so there is exactly one number here to argue about\n-- and no second one to fall out of step with it.\n--\n-- The scale to judge them against is the note above Config.PocketMoney: a whole\n-- childhood of allowance comes to somewhere near seven thousand, and that same\n-- note puts a teenager doing nothing at about 2,400 a year before their allowance\n-- would start out-earning a first job. So a first job pays 2,400 -- worth having,\n-- not life-changing -- and adult work pays 8,000, which is the first income in a\n-- life that is actually a living.\n\nlocal ReplicatedStorage = game:GetService(\"ReplicatedStorage\")\nlocal Config = require(ReplicatedStorage:WaitForChild(\"Config\"))\nlocal Types = require(ReplicatedStorage:WaitForChild(\"Types\"))\n\n-- Content asking content a question. The only thing this wants from People is whether\n-- the person a job names as a reference actually exists, and that has to be answered at\n-- load: a vouch pointing at nobody is a second route into a job that quietly never opens.\nlocal People = require(script.Parent.People)\n\ntype Job = Types.Job\n\nlocal SOURCE: { Job } = {\n	{\n		id = \"shop_assistant\",\n		title = \"Shop assistant\",\n		-- The corner shop, where the till and the aisles are.\n		placeId = \"corner_shop\",\n		-- Sixteen, which is the age this whole system was specified around: the\n		-- first year you can trade your time for money instead of being given it.\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n		shiftEvents = {\n			{ id = \"shop_assistant.rude_customer\", weight = 3, line = \"A customer is complaining about the price. What do you do?\", money = -20, happiness = -5 },\n			{ id = \"shop_assistant.helpful\", weight = 4, line = \"An elderly customer needs help reaching a shelf. You fetch it for them.\", standing = 1, happiness = 3 },\n			{ id = \"shop_assistant.busy_lunch\", weight = 2, line = \"The lunch rush hits all at once. You keep your head.\", standing = 1, money = 10 },\n			{ id = \"shop_assistant.found_money\", weight = 1, line = \"You find a forgotten five-dollar bill under the counter.\", money = 5 },\n			{ id = \"shop_assistant.takeaway\", weight = 3, line = \"A customer places a takeaway order. You fetch the items and bag them.\", standing = 1, money = 5 },\n		},\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"office_clerk\",\n		title = \"Office clerk\",\n		-- Upstairs, through the stairwell we measured headroom on.\n		placeId = \"office\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 8000,\n		-- The staircase is the career, and this is the handrail. Turning eighteen is\n		-- not enough on its own: the office wants to know what the shop floor made of\n		-- you, which is the difference between a career and a birthday.\n		--\n		-- Six is the bottom of \"well thought of\" -- three or four shifts answered like\n		-- someone who cared, or a full year of simply turning up and doing it. Priced\n		-- so that a player who worked at sixteen walks up the stairs at eighteen and\n		-- finds the door open, and a player who spent two years walking out early\n		-- finds it shut and knows exactly why.\n		requires = { jobId = \"shop_assistant\", standing = 6 },\n		-- What school was for, and the only place in the game that reads smarts.\n		--\n		-- Priced off what a childhood actually does to the number. Everyone starts at\n		-- 50. Twelve years of sitting lessons and answering most of them puts a child\n		-- somewhere near the ceiling; twelve years of never turning up costs two a year\n		-- and leaves them in the twenties. Sixty is set just above the line a player\n		-- who did nothing could reach by standing still, and well below what anybody who\n		-- went at all will have -- five good lessons out of a possible thirty-six clears\n		-- it. It is not a wall for the diligent. It is a door that is shut, at eighteen,\n		-- for the player who spent their childhood elsewhere, and the game will have\n		-- told them the number on the office window for years by then.\n		minSmarts = 60,\n		-- And the way in that is not a classroom.\n		--\n		-- Mr. Alderman stands outside the shop the ladder starts at, and this is what\n		-- twenty years of being decent to him is worth: he knows the office, and if he\n		-- thinks well enough of you he says so. Forty-five is \"close\" -- roughly a decade\n		-- of turning up -- and fifteen brings the bar down to forty-five smarts, which is\n		-- a childhood with some school in it rather than none.\n		--\n		-- Priced so it is a second route and never a shortcut. A player who skipped every\n		-- lesson lands in the twenties and is still short even with him vouching, so the\n		-- door does not open on friendship alone; a player who went occasionally and knew\n		-- their neighbor gets in where either on its own would not have been enough. That\n		-- is the whole reason both systems exist in the same childhood.\n		vouch = { personId = \"neighbor\", minBond = 45, worthSmarts = 15 },\n		shiftEvents = {\n			{ id = \"office_clerk.filing_error\", weight = 3, line = \"You spot a filing error that could cost the company. Do you report it?\", standing = 2 },\n			{ id = \"office_clerk.overwork\", weight = 2, line = \"The boss piles on extra paperwork at 4pm. You stay late to finish it.\", happiness = -5, standing = 1 },\n			{ id = \"office_clerk.good_report\", weight = 2, line = \"Your quarterly report impressed the manager.\", standing = 2, money = 50 },\n			{ id = \"office_clerk.paper Jam\", weight = 4, line = \"The copier jams during a busy morning. You clear it without being asked.\", standing = 1 },\n		},\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"manager\",\n		title = \"Manager\",\n		-- Same building, different floor. The manager's office is upstairs from\n		-- the clerk's, with a door that does not open until the office has had\n		-- enough of you to trust you with the rest.\n		placeId = \"office\",\n		-- Two years of proving the clerk was not a fluke. Standing 14 is \"relied\n		-- on\" -- four good shifts a year for three years, or two years of simply\n		-- turning up and doing it properly.\n		minAge = 20,\n		-- Double the clerk. The first salary in the game that changes what a life\n		-- looks like rather than what a week feels like.\n		yearlySalary = 16000,\n		-- The standing gate is the real door. Fourteen is the band where the office\n		-- knows your name and what you can do. No vouch here: the office has had\n		-- two years to make up its mind, and a word from someone outside it does\n		-- not carry further than that.\n		requires = { jobId = \"office_clerk\", standing = 14 },\n		-- The top of what a childhood with some school in it reaches, and well\n		-- below what a childhood full of it produces. The manager has to read a\n		-- budget and talk to a client, which is not the same as being clever but\n		-- is close enough that the number sits here rather than lower.\n		minSmarts = 75,\n		shiftEvents = {\n			{ id = \"manager.team_conflict\", weight = 2, line = \"Two clerks are arguing over shift coverage. You mediate.\", standing = 2, happiness = 2 },\n			{ id = \"manager.quarterly_review\", weight = 1, line = \"Quarterly review: the numbers are solid. Your bonus reflects it.\", money = 200, standing = 3 },\n			{ id = \"manager.staff_shortage\", weight = 3, line = \"Two people called in sick. You cover the floor yourself.\", happiness = -5, standing = 1 },\n		},\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"bakery\",\n		title = \"Baker\",\n		-- The bread shop down the far street, and the second door a sixteen-year-old\n		-- can walk through. Same rung as the shop assistant on purpose: the first\n		-- job in a life should be a choice between two rooms, not a single sentence.\n		placeId = \"bakery\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		-- The same wage as the shop floor, for the same reason: what the first job\n		-- teaches is that you are worth something, not what you are worth.\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n		shiftEvents = {\n			{ id = \"bakery.early_start\", weight = 3, line = \"The oven needs tending at 5am. You're here before dawn.\", standing = 1 },\n			{ id = \"bakery.wasted_flour\", weight = 2, line = \"A batch of bread went stale. You dispose of it and start fresh.\", money = -15 },\n			{ id = \"bakery.perfect_loaf\", weight = 1, line = \"The sourdough came out perfect. A customer asks for the recipe.\", standing = 2, happiness = 5 },\n			{ id = \"bakery.busy_morning\", weight = 3, line = \"The breakfast rush never stops. Your arms ache but the display stays full.\", standing = 1, money = 10 },\n		},\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"librarian\",\n		title = \"Librarian\",\n		-- The reading room up the far road. The smarts route out of the bakery: it\n		-- pays more than the shop's office door for less standing, because the thing\n		-- it is really asking for is that school happened.\n		placeId = \"library\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6000,\n		-- Five is \"well thought of\" -- a single year of turning up at the bakery and\n		-- doing the job. The ladder is two years long here rather than the office's\n		-- three, because a baker's trade is smaller than a shop floor's and this is\n		-- the quiet rung, not the top.\n		requires = { jobId = \"bakery\", standing = 5 },\n		-- The line school was always drawing. Fifty-five is a childhood with most\n		-- of its lessons in it -- well clear of the twenty-somethings who never went,\n		-- and below what a diligent child clears before they are fifteen. A librarian\n		-- is not a genius. They are somebody who reads, which is close enough.\n		minSmarts = 55,\n		shiftEvents = {\n			{ id = \"librarian.misfiled\", weight = 3, line = \"A book is misfiled and a patron can't find it. You help locate it.\", standing = 1, happiness = 2 },\n			{ id = \"librarian.quiet_afternoon\", weight = 2, line = \"A rare quiet afternoon. You get behind on reshelfing.\", standing = 1 },\n			{ id = \"librarian.damaged_book\", weight = 1, line = \"A patron damaged a reference book. You process the replacement cost.\", money = -25 },\n			{ id = \"librarian.children_hour\", weight = 3, line = \"Story time with the kids goes well. They ask when you're coming back.\", happiness = 5 },\n		},\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"mechanic\",\n		title = \"Mechanic\",\n		-- The garage on the far side of the bakery, and the practical route out of\n		-- it: the same standing door the librarian walks through, opened instead by\n		-- working hands. This is the route that keeps a life whose smarts never\n		-- reached the library's bar from ending at sixteen.\n		placeId = \"garage\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6400,\n		requires = { jobId = \"bakery\", standing = 5 },\n		-- Where the practical route lives. Forty-five is a childhood with a bit of\n		-- school in it rather than none -- nobody who skipped every lesson for a\n		-- decade clears it -- but it is well below the library's bar, because the\n		-- garage will take the hands it is offered and train the rest.\n		minSmarts = 45,\n		-- The ladder man vouches, in a way that rhymes with his own story. Mr.\n		-- Alderman's gutter comes away in summer and he holds no ladder steady, but\n		-- he knows who keeps this town's cars and its rooflines working, and if he\n		-- thinks well enough of you he says so. Worth less than his word to the\n		-- office -- fifteen years of seeing you about the street is not the same as\n		-- recommending you for the room where the money is -- but it will pull a\n		-- player who half-went to school over the garage's lower line.\n		vouch = { personId = \"neighbor\", minBond = 40, worthSmarts = 12 },\n		shiftEvents = {\n			{ id = \"mechanic.tough_job\", weight = 3, line = \"An oldtimer's car needs more than expected. You talk them through it honestly.\", standing = 2, happiness = 3 },\n			{ id = \"mechanic.parts_delay\", weight = 2, line = \"The parts order is late. You wait around doing inventory.\", happiness = -3 },\n			{ id = \"mechanic.fix_it_right\", weight = 2, line = \"You caught a safety issue another shop missed. The customer is grateful.\", standing = 2, money = 30 },\n			{ id = \"mechanic.oily_break\", weight = 3, line = \"A coffee break with the other mechanic. Good banter.\", happiness = 3 },\n		},\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"gym_instructor\",\n		title = \"Gym instructor\",\n		-- The gym at the north end, and the only job in the game that is earned as\n		-- much in the body as on the record. It sits off the mechanic's rung because\n		-- the garage is where this town's physical work already happened; this is\n		-- where it starts being paid to happen to other people.\n		placeId = \"gym\",\n		minAge = 20,\n		yearlySalary = 8000,\n		-- Ten is \"relied on\" -- roughly two good years at the garage. Being asked to\n		-- run a room full of people who are hurting on purpose requires having done\n		-- that yourself for a while.\n		requires = { jobId = \"mechanic\", standing = 10 },\n		-- Below the library's and clinic's bars, because the gym does not need to\n		-- read a budget. It needs somebody who knows a movement from a mistake, and\n		-- the way this game checks for that is that school happened at all.\n		minSmarts = 50,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"clinic_assistant\",\n		title = \"Clinic assistant\",\n		-- The clinic on the south road. The top of the services row and the care\n		-- route: the best-paid job in the game that does not sit upstairs in the\n		-- office, and the one that asks the most of a childhood.\n		placeId = \"clinic\",\n		minAge = 20,\n		yearlySalary = 12000,\n		-- Twelve is \"relied on\" at the library -- two years of being the quiet rung\n		-- this ladder is built on. There is no vouch and no shortcut: the clinic\n		-- takes the people the library proved it could trust with other people's\n		-- information, which is most of what a waiting room is.\n		requires = { jobId = \"librarian\", standing = 12 },\n		-- The top of what a childhood produces outside the office. Sixty-five is a\n		-- childhood with most of its lessons in it and a library career on top, and\n		-- it is the one bar on this row that a player who skipped school simply\n		-- cannot reach from the bakery -- which is the point of the row.\n		minSmarts = 65,\n	},\n\n	-- ------------------------------------------------------------------\n	-- City careers.\n	--\n	-- The five ladders below grow out of the town's two first jobs: the shop\n	-- floor's shelf work and a baker's shift are the two things this city\n	-- already trusts, so they are the rungs the new rows stand on. The\n	-- independent jobs are the city's fresh first rungs -- a sixteen-year-old\n	-- should walk a street of open doors, not two sentences -- and they pay\n	-- what a first job pays.\n	--\n	-- Retail ladder: shop floor -> specialist shops -> the market's manager.\n	{\n		id = \"clothing_retail\",\n		title = \"Clothing retail\",\n		placeId = \"clothing_store\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6000,\n		-- The same six shifts the shop floor asked for, because the city's\n		-- shops are that ladder continuing rather than a fresh one.\n		requires = { jobId = \"shop_assistant\", standing = 6 },\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"electronics_retail\",\n		title = \"Electronics retail\",\n		placeId = \"electronics\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6400,\n		requires = { jobId = \"shop_assistant\", standing = 6 },\n		-- Selling a thing you have to be able to explain. Fifty-five is the\n		-- library's bar: a childhood with most of its lessons in it, which is\n		-- what it takes to answer a question about what you are selling.\n		minSmarts = 55,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"supermarket_manager\",\n		title = \"Supermarket manager\",\n		placeId = \"supermarket\",\n		minAge = 20,\n		yearlySalary = 16000,\n		-- The market is the retail row's office, and it trusts the same way\n		-- the office does: fourteen is \"relied on\", the door a building with a\n		-- dozen tills opens to somebody it knows by name.\n		requires = { jobId = \"clothing_retail\", standing = 14 },\n		minSmarts = 70,\n	},\n\n	-- Food ladder: the bakery and the cafe are both first rungs; the pizza\n	-- kitchen and the restaurant floor grow out of them, and the chef is the\n	-- top of the row -- the clinic's pay for a kitchen's version of trust.\n	{\n		id = \"barista\",\n		title = \"Barista\",\n		placeId = \"cafe\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6000,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"pizzeria_cook\",\n		title = \"Pizzeria cook\",\n		placeId = \"pizzeria\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6400,\n		requires = { jobId = \"barista\", standing = 5 },\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"restaurant_server\",\n		title = \"Restaurant server\",\n		placeId = \"restaurant\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6000,\n		-- The bakery teaches what carrying a tray to somebody who is hungry\n		-- takes: five shifts, the same door the librarian and the mechanic\n		-- walk through off the same rung.\n		requires = { jobId = \"bakery\", standing = 5 },\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"chef\",\n		title = \"Chef\",\n		placeId = \"dining_1\",\n		minAge = 20,\n		yearlySalary = 12000,\n		requires = { jobId = \"pizzeria_cook\", standing = 12 },\n		-- A kitchen reads a menu, not a budget, so the bar sits with the\n		-- librarian's rather than the office's: a childhood with most of its\n		-- lessons in it, and a career of cooking on top.\n		minSmarts = 55,\n	},\n\n	-- Service ladder: the post office sorts, the bank counts, and the hotel\n	-- runs the front desk that both of those earn.\n	{\n		id = \"postal_worker\",\n		title = \"Postal worker\",\n		placeId = \"post_office\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6000,\n		requires = { jobId = \"bakery\", standing = 5 },\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"bank_teller\",\n		title = \"Bank teller\",\n		placeId = \"bank\",\n		minAge = 20,\n		yearlySalary = 8000,\n		requires = { jobId = \"postal_worker\", standing = 8 },\n		-- The bar is here because a teller has to be trusted to add up.\n		-- Fifty-five is a childhood with most of its lessons in it.\n		minSmarts = 55,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"hotel_receptionist\",\n		title = \"Hotel receptionist\",\n		placeId = \"hotel\",\n		minAge = 20,\n		yearlySalary = 8000,\n		requires = { jobId = \"bank_teller\", standing = 8 },\n	},\n\n	-- Health ladder: the pharmacy is the door into care, and the dental\n	-- chair and the vet's table both stand on it.\n	{\n		id = \"pharmacy_tech\",\n		title = \"Pharmacy technician\",\n		placeId = \"pharmacy\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6000,\n		requires = { jobId = \"shop_assistant\", standing = 5 },\n		-- A shelf you hand medicine off needs to be read properly. Fifty is\n		-- the mechanic's bar: a childhood with some school in it rather than\n		-- none, because the ones who skipped every lesson must be the ones\n		-- who never get the job.\n		minSmarts = 50,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"dental_assistant\",\n		title = \"Dental assistant\",\n		placeId = \"dental\",\n		minAge = 20,\n		yearlySalary = 8000,\n		requires = { jobId = \"pharmacy_tech\", standing = 8 },\n		minSmarts = 60,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"vet_tech\",\n		title = \"Vet technician\",\n		placeId = \"vet\",\n		minAge = 20,\n		yearlySalary = 8000,\n		requires = { jobId = \"pharmacy_tech\", standing = 8 },\n		minSmarts = 55,\n	},\n\n	-- Safety ladder: the site and the warehouse are the hands-first route,\n	-- and the station and the precinct are where it ends up trusted.\n	{\n		id = \"construction_worker\",\n		title = \"Construction worker\",\n		placeId = \"construction_site\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6400,\n		requires = { jobId = \"bakery\", standing = 5 },\n		shiftEvents = {\n			{ id = \"construction_worker.safety_check\", weight = 3, line = \"Safety inspector comes through. Everything passes on the first try.\", standing = 2 },\n			{ id = \"construction_worker.bad_weather\", weight = 2, line = \"Rain delays the pour. You wait it out on the scaffolding.\", happiness = -5 },\n			{ id = \"construction_worker.lift\", weight = 2, line = \"You helped a coworker lift a heavy beam. Teamwork counts.\", standing = 1, happiness = 3 },\n			{ id = \"construction_worker.early_finish\", weight = 1, line = \"The wall went up ahead of schedule. Foreman lets you clock out early.\", money = 20 },\n		},\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"warehouse_worker\",\n		title = \"Warehouse worker\",\n		placeId = \"warehouse\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6400,\n		requires = { jobId = \"construction_worker\", standing = 6 },\n		shiftEvents = {\n			{ id = \"warehouse_worker.inv_count\", weight = 3, line = \"Inventory count is off by a few units. You reconcile it.\", standing = 1 },\n			{ id = \"warehouse_worker.heavy_lift\", weight = 2, line = \"You moved a pallet that was supposed to be two people. Back aches.\", happiness = -3, standing = 1 },\n			{ id = \"warehouse_worker.found_good\", weight = 2, line = \"You organized a messy aisle. The team thanks you.\", standing = 2, happiness = 3 },\n			{ id = \"warehouse_worker.scanner_broke\", weight = 1, line = \"The scanner broke mid-shift. You hand-wrote the logs.\", happiness = -5 },\n		},\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"firefighter\",\n		title = \"Firefighter\",\n		placeId = \"fire_station\",\n		minAge = 20,\n		yearlySalary = 12000,\n		requires = { jobId = \"warehouse_worker\", standing = 10 },\n		minSmarts = 60,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"police_officer\",\n		title = \"Police officer\",\n		placeId = \"police_station\",\n		minAge = 20,\n		yearlySalary = 12000,\n		requires = { jobId = \"warehouse_worker\", standing = 10 },\n		-- The highest bar on the safety row: a childhood with most of its\n		-- lessons in it. Mr. Alderman's word pulls it down but never to the\n		-- floor -- fifty-three still needs most of school.\n		minSmarts = 65,\n		vouch = { personId = \"neighbor\", minBond = 45, worthSmarts = 12 },\n	},\n\n	-- The city's fresh first jobs. Sixteen in the city is a street of open\n	-- doors, and these are the doors.\n	{\n		id = \"mall_cashier\",\n		title = \"Mall cashier\",\n		placeId = \"mall_entrance\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"florist\",\n		title = \"Florist\",\n		placeId = \"florist\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"bookseller\",\n		title = \"Bookseller\",\n		placeId = \"bookstore\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"toy_seller\",\n		title = \"Toy seller\",\n		placeId = \"toy_store\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"music_seller\",\n		title = \"Music seller\",\n		placeId = \"music_store\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"laundry_worker\",\n		title = \"Laundry worker\",\n		placeId = \"laundromat\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"gas_attendant\",\n		title = \"Gas station attendant\",\n		placeId = \"gas_station\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"car_wash_worker\",\n		title = \"Car wash attendant\",\n		placeId = \"car_wash\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"cinema_usher\",\n		title = \"Cinema usher\",\n		placeId = \"cinema\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"bowling_attendant\",\n		title = \"Bowling attendant\",\n		placeId = \"bowling\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"arcade_worker\",\n		title = \"Arcade worker\",\n		placeId = \"arcade\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"farm_hand\",\n		title = \"Farm hand\",\n		placeId = \"farm\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n	},\n\n	-- The eighteen-year-old trades the city keeps off the ladders: work that\n	-- asks for a finished childhood but does not sit on anybody's row.\n	{\n		id = \"barber\",\n		title = \"Barber\",\n		placeId = \"barbershop\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6000,\n		-- A trade a childhood with a bit of school in it can reach, and the\n		-- one place Mr. Alderman's word reaches into this row: he knows who\n		-- keeps a decent pair of scissors, and his say-so pulls the bar down\n		-- to thirty -- a childhood with some school in it rather than none.\n		minSmarts = 40,\n		vouch = { personId = \"neighbor\", minBond = 35, worthSmarts = 10 },\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"hairdresser\",\n		title = \"Hairdresser\",\n		placeId = \"salon\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6000,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"tattoo_artist\",\n		title = \"Tattoo artist\",\n		placeId = \"tattoo_parlor\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6400,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"car_salesperson\",\n		title = \"Car salesperson\",\n		placeId = \"auto_dealer\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 8000,\n		-- The commission side of the job needs the reading a price list and a\n		-- finance sheet take, so the bar sits with the bank's rather than the\n		-- barber's: a childhood with most of its lessons in it.\n		minSmarts = 55,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"town_clerk\",\n		title = \"Town clerk\",\n		placeId = \"town_hall\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 8000,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"referee\",\n		title = \"Sports referee\",\n		placeId = \"soccer_field\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6400,\n	},\n	-- Work-from-home jobs. Each one is anchored to a suburb house the player can\n	-- buy; the placeId is the house id, not a public building. A life must own\n	-- the house and link the job before it shows up in the job posts.\n	{\n		id = \"freelance_writer\",\n		title = \"Freelance writer\",\n		placeId = \"suburb_1\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n		minSmarts = 50,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"home_accountant\",\n		title = \"Home accountant\",\n		placeId = \"suburb_5\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6000,\n		minSmarts = 60,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"online_teacher\",\n		title = \"Online tutor\",\n		placeId = \"suburb_10\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 6000,\n		minSmarts = 55,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"remote_coder\",\n		title = \"Remote developer\",\n		placeId = \"suburb_50\",\n		minAge = 20,\n		yearlySalary = 12000,\n		minSmarts = 70,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"home_healthcare\",\n		title = \"Home healthcare aid\",\n		placeId = \"suburb_20\",\n		minAge = 18,\n		yearlySalary = 8000,\n		minSmarts = 55,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"house_sitter\",\n		title = \"House sitter\",\n		placeId = \"suburb_30\",\n		minAge = 16,\n		yearlySalary = 2400,\n		minSmarts = 45,\n	},\n}\n\nlocal all: { Job } = {}\nlocal byId: { [string]: Job } = {}\n\n-- Content mistakes are authoring bugs, not runtime conditions, so these throw at\n-- start rather than producing a job that quietly pays nothing and is never missed.\nlocal function validate(job: Job)\n	if byId[job.id] ~= nil then\n		error(`[AGES content] duplicate job id \"{job.id}\"`)\n	end\n	if job.title == \"\" then\n		error(`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" has no title; it is what the player is told they do`)\n	end\n	if job.placeId == \"\" then\n		error(`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" names no place, so there is nowhere to clock on`)\n	end\n	if job.minAge < 0 then\n		error(`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" has a negative minimum age`)\n	end\n\n	-- Whole units above zero. A salary of zero is a job that pays nothing, which\n	-- is a content mistake rather than a design; a fractional one would be rounded\n	-- somewhere downstream and silently stop adding up to the figure authored here.\n	if job.yearlySalary <= 0 or job.yearlySalary ~= math.floor(job.yearlySalary) then\n		error(`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" pays {job.yearlySalary} a year; a salary must be a whole number above zero`)\n	end\n\n	-- A smarts gate has to sit inside the range smarts can actually take, or it is a\n	-- door with no key: above the ceiling nobody clears it however hard they worked,\n	-- and at or below the floor everybody does and the gate is decoration.\n	local minSmarts = job.minSmarts\n	if minSmarts ~= nil then\n		local stats = Config.Stats\n		if minSmarts <= stats.Min or minSmarts > stats.Max then\n			error(\n				`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" asks for {minSmarts} smarts; it must be above the floor of `\n					.. `{stats.Min} and no more than the ceiling of {stats.Max}`\n			)\n		end\n	end\n\n	local vouch = job.vouch\n	if vouch ~= nil then\n		if People.ById[vouch.personId] == nil then\n			error(`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" is vouched for by \"{vouch.personId}\", who is not a person`)\n		end\n		-- A vouch with no bar to lower is a field that does nothing, which is worse than\n		-- a missing feature: it reads as a route into the job that silently is not one.\n		if minSmarts == nil then\n			error(`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" has a vouch but asks for no smarts, so there is nothing to lower`)\n		end\n		local people = Config.People\n		if vouch.minBond <= people.MinBond or vouch.minBond > people.MaxBond then\n			error(\n				`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" wants a bond of {vouch.minBond}, which is either below the `\n					.. `floor of {people.MinBond} -- so everybody is vouched for -- or above the ceiling of `\n					.. `{people.MaxBond}, so nobody ever is`\n			)\n		end\n		-- Has to move the bar and must not move it to the floor. The first would be a\n		-- reference nobody notices; the second a job that could be had on a friendship\n		-- and no education at all, which is exactly what the bar is there to prevent.\n		if vouch.worthSmarts <= 0 or minSmarts - vouch.worthSmarts <= Config.Stats.Min then\n			error(\n				`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" values a word from \"{vouch.personId}\" at {vouch.worthSmarts} `\n					.. `smarts against a bar of {minSmarts}; it must be worth something and must leave a bar behind`\n			)\n		end\n	end\n\n	-- The one check that earns its keep. An hour's pay is the salary divided by the\n	-- year's game hours, and that division has to come out whole or every hour\n	-- worked pays a rounded figure and a full year of work quietly fails to add up\n	-- to the salary printed above -- by a few dollars, invisibly, forever.\n	if job.yearlySalary % Config.Work.YearGameHours ~= 0 then\n		error(\n			`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" pays {job.yearlySalary} a year, which does not divide evenly `\n				.. `by the {Config.Work.YearGameHours} game hours in a working year; `\n				.. `a year of work would not add up to the salary`\n		)\n	end\nend\n\nfor _, job in SOURCE do\n	validate(job)\n	byId[job.id] = job\n	table.insert(all, job)\nend\n\n-- Prerequisites are checked in a second pass, because a job is allowed to require\n-- one declared below it and the first pass has only seen what came before.\nlocal function validateRequirement(job: Job)\n	local requirement = job.requires\n	if requirement == nil then\n		return\n	end\n\n	if requirement.jobId == job.id then\n		error(`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" requires standing at itself, which cannot be earned without it`)\n	end\n\n	local prerequisite = byId[requirement.jobId]\n	if prerequisite == nil then\n		error(`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" requires \"{requirement.jobId}\", which is not a job`)\n	end\n\n	-- Above the floor, or the requirement is met by everyone who has never worked a\n	-- day and the gate is decoration. At or below the ceiling, or it can never be\n	-- met by anyone and the job is unreachable -- which at runtime looks exactly\n	-- like a door that is simply broken.\n	local standing = Config.Work.Standing\n	if requirement.standing <= 0 or requirement.standing > standing.Max then\n		error(\n			`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" requires standing {requirement.standing} at `\n				.. `\"{requirement.jobId}\"; it must be above 0 and no more than the ceiling of {standing.Max}`\n		)\n	end\n\n	-- A job you can only qualify for by already holding something further up the\n	-- ladder is a job nobody can ever reach. Walked rather than checked one deep,\n	-- because a three-job loop is just as unreachable and far harder to spot by eye.\n	local seen: { [string]: boolean } = { [job.id] = true }\n	local step: Job? = prerequisite\n	while step ~= nil do\n		if seen[step.id] then\n			error(`[AGES content] job \"{job.id}\" sits in a loop of requirements, so nothing in it can ever be taken`)\n		end\n		seen[step.id] = true\n		local nextRequirement = step.requires\n		step = if nextRequirement ~= nil then byId[nextRequirement.jobId] else nil\n	end\nend\n\nlocal hasEntryPoint = false\nfor _, job in all do\n	validateRequirement(job)\n	if job.requires == nil then\n		hasEntryPoint = true\n	end\nend\n\n-- Somewhere there has to be a job that asks nothing of you but your age, or the\n-- ladder has no bottom rung and no player can ever start working at all. The one\n-- content mistake here that would leave a completely silent game: no errors, no\n-- warnings, just a world where clocking on never happens anywhere.\nif not hasEntryPoint then\n	error(\"[AGES content] every job requires standing at another one; there is no job anybody could take first\")\nend\n\nlocal Jobs = {\n	All = all,\n	ById = byId,\n}\n\n-- What one game hour of this job pays, in whole units. The only place a salary is\n-- ever divided, so the arithmetic exists once.\nfunction Jobs.HourlyRate(job: Job): number\n	return job.yearlySalary // Config.Work.YearGameHours\nend\n\n-- The lowest age at which any job at all can be held. Read off the content rather\n-- than written down a second time in Config, so adding a job for a younger worker\n-- moves it automatically.\nfunction Jobs.MinimumWorkingAge(): number\n	local lowest = math.huge\n	for _, job in all do\n		lowest = math.min(lowest, job.minAge)\n	end\n	return lowest\nend\n\nreturn Jobs\n"
table.insert(AGES_MANIFEST, "src/server/content/Jobs.luau")
AGES_SOURCES["src/server/content/Lessons.luau"] = "--!strict\n-- What school actually asks you.\n--\n-- Content, and a table rather than code for the reason JobTasks gives: a lesson is\n-- five questions and a childhood is thirty-six lessons, so the thing that has to stay\n-- cheap is writing another one. A question is four lines here and never touches the\n-- loop that asks it.\n--\n-- Unlike a shift's tasks, these are not all the same thing in different words. A task\n-- is a place to walk to and the words are decoration; a question has a right answer,\n-- and it is the only thing in this game a player can be wrong about. Everything else\n-- a life event asks you has three answers and three different lives afterwards. This\n-- has three answers and one of them is simply correct, which is what makes a lesson\n-- something you can be good at.\n--\n-- What is deliberately *not* here:\n--\n--   No syllabus, and no subjects. A child in this game is not being taught math and\n--   then geography -- they are in a room with a board, being asked things. Sorting\n--   these into subjects would buy a menu nobody asked for and would make every gap in\n--   the bank look like a missing feature rather than a question not written yet.\n--\n--   That one is now overturned, and the paragraph above is left standing because the\n--   reasoning in it was correct and stopped being correct for a reason worth recording.\n--   It holds for exactly as long as every subject is the same act. Sorting one\n--   multiple-choice question from another by subject really does buy nothing. Science\n--   is no longer a multiple-choice question -- it is a procedure you walk, in a room\n--   with benches in it -- and once one subject is a different thing to do with your\n--   hands, the others need names to be told apart by. Types.SchoolSubject is the\n--   timetable that resulted; content/Curriculum.luau is the list.\n--\n--   This bank did not become obsolete when that happened. It is what every subject\n--   whose verb is not written yet still runs on, and the entries here are no longer\n--   the whole of school -- they are the \"quiz\" verb, which is a placeholder with a\n--   design attached and is meant to end up serving nothing.\n--\n--   No trick questions and no trivia. A question whose answer you either happen to\n--   know or do not is a coin toss with extra reading. Every one of these is either\n--   something a player can work out from the question, or something worth knowing --\n--   and the `because` line means the ones they could not work out are worth getting\n--   wrong once.\n--\n--   Nothing an eight-year-old at a keyboard would find upsetting or would need an\n--   adult for. This is a game rated for everyone and school is the part of it most\n--   likely to be read over a shoulder.\n--\n-- The age window on each question is what stops the whole bank being one room. A\n-- seven-year-old is asked what half of twenty is and a sixteen-year-old is asked what\n-- a deficit is, and a game that asked them the same thing would be admitting the nine\n-- years in between did not happen. The bands overlap on purpose -- eleven is a\n-- perfectly good age to still be asked how many sides a triangle has, and a hard\n-- boundary would make a birthday feel like a different game.\n\nlocal ReplicatedStorage = game:GetService(\"ReplicatedStorage\")\n\nlocal Config = require(ReplicatedStorage:WaitForChild(\"Config\"))\nlocal Types = require(ReplicatedStorage:WaitForChild(\"Types\"))\n\ntype LessonQuestion = Types.LessonQuestion\n\nlocal Lessons = {}\n\n-- Read by the validator and nowhere else. Both come from Config rather than being\n-- restated, so the authoring limit and the limit the lesson actually enforces can\n-- never drift: a question with five answers in a game that paints four pads is a\n-- question with an answer you cannot reach.\nlocal MAX_ANSWERS = Config.School.MaxAnswers\nlocal MAX_ASK = Config.School.MaxAskLength\n-- The floor on how many answers a question may offer. Two, because a question with one\n-- answer is a sentence with a pad under it.\nlocal MIN_ANSWERS = 2\n\n-- Written as a teacher would say them out loud, because that is the register the\n-- `because` line has to hold: it is the one sentence in this system that is neither an\n-- instruction nor a score, and it is read by somebody who has just got it wrong.\nlocal SOURCE: { LessonQuestion } = {\n	-- The early years. Arithmetic you can do on your fingers, words you have seen\n	-- written down, and facts about the world that are true outside the game too.\n	{\n		id = \"add_small\",\n		ask = \"What is 2 + 3?\",\n		answers = { \"4\", \"5\", \"6\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Two, and then three more, makes five.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"subtract_small\",\n		ask = \"What is 10 - 4?\",\n		answers = { \"5\", \"6\", \"7\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Take four away from ten and six is left.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"triangle_sides\",\n		ask = \"How many sides does a triangle have?\",\n		answers = { \"Three\", \"Four\", \"Six\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Three. That is what the tri in triangle means.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 10,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"color_mix\",\n		ask = \"Blue and yellow mixed together make what?\",\n		answers = { \"Green\", \"Purple\", \"Orange\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Blue and yellow make green.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"days_week\",\n		ask = \"How many days are there in a week?\",\n		answers = { \"Five\", \"Seven\", \"Ten\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Seven, Monday all the way around to Sunday.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"months_year\",\n		ask = \"How many months are there in a year?\",\n		answers = { \"Ten\", \"Twelve\", \"Twenty\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Twelve, January through December.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"spell_friend\",\n		ask = \"Which of these is spelled correctly?\",\n		answers = { \"freind\", \"friend\", \"frend\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Friend. The i comes before the e.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 10,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"spider_legs\",\n		ask = \"How many legs does a spider have?\",\n		answers = { \"Six\", \"Eight\", \"Ten\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Eight. Insects have six legs, and a spider is not an insect.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 10,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"water_cold\",\n		ask = \"What happens to water when it gets very cold?\",\n		answers = { \"It freezes\", \"It boils\", \"It disappears\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"It freezes, and turns into ice.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"biggest_number\",\n		ask = \"Which of these numbers is the biggest?\",\n		answers = { \"19\", \"91\", \"9\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"91. The nine is in the tens column, so it counts for ninety.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 10,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"sun_direction\",\n		ask = \"Which way does the sun come up in the morning?\",\n		answers = { \"The east\", \"The west\", \"The north\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"The sun rises in the east and sets in the west.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 10,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"money_add\",\n		ask = \"You have $6 and you are given $4. How much do you have?\",\n		answers = { \"$8\", \"$10\", \"$12\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Six and four make ten.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"not_animal\",\n		ask = \"Which of these is not an animal?\",\n		answers = { \"A rabbit\", \"An oak tree\", \"A sparrow\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"An oak is a tree, and a tree is a plant.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"half_twenty\",\n		ask = \"What is half of 20?\",\n		answers = { \"5\", \"10\", \"15\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Twenty split into two equal halves gives ten each.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 10,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"opposite_heavy\",\n		ask = \"What is the opposite of heavy?\",\n		answers = { \"Light\", \"Tall\", \"Loud\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Light is the opposite of heavy.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"baby_cat\",\n		ask = \"What is a baby cat called?\",\n		answers = { \"A kitten\", \"A puppy\", \"A cub\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"A kitten. A puppy is a baby dog.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"plural_mouse\",\n		ask = \"What do you call more than one mouse?\",\n		answers = { \"Mouses\", \"Mice\", \"Mouse\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Mice. English does not always just add an s.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 10,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"brush_teeth\",\n		ask = \"Why do you brush your teeth?\",\n		answers = { \"To stop them rotting\", \"To make them grow\", \"To make them sharper\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Brushing takes away what would otherwise rot them.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n\n	-- The middle years. Arithmetic with a step in it, and the first questions whose\n	-- answer is a thing you were told rather than a thing you can count.\n	{\n		id = \"times_seven_eight\",\n		ask = \"What is 7 x 8?\",\n		answers = { \"54\", \"56\", \"64\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Fifty-six. Seven eights are fifty-six.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"continent\",\n		ask = \"Which of these is a continent?\",\n		answers = { \"Africa\", \"Egypt\", \"The Nile\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Africa is a continent. Egypt is a country in it.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"closest_planet\",\n		ask = \"Which planet is closest to the Sun?\",\n		answers = { \"Earth\", \"Mercury\", \"Mars\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Mercury. It is the innermost of the planets.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"bigger_fraction\",\n		ask = \"Which is bigger, one half or one third?\",\n		answers = { \"One half\", \"One third\", \"They are the same\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Cut a cake in two and your piece is bigger than if you cut it in three.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"which_verb\",\n		ask = \"Which of these words is a verb?\",\n		answers = { \"Quickly\", \"Running\", \"Bright\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"A verb is something being done, and running is being done.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"capital_france\",\n		ask = \"What is the capital of France?\",\n		answers = { \"Paris\", \"Rome\", \"Madrid\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Paris. Rome is Italy's and Madrid is Spain's.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"plant_food\",\n		ask = \"What do plants use to make their own food?\",\n		answers = { \"Sunlight\", \"Moonlight\", \"Wind\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Sunlight. The word for it is photosynthesis.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"states_matter\",\n		ask = \"Ice, water and steam are one substance in three what?\",\n		answers = { \"Colors\", \"States\", \"Weights\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"States. Solid, liquid and gas are the three states of matter.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"square_perimeter\",\n		ask = \"A square has sides of 4. How far is it all the way around?\",\n		answers = { \"8\", \"12\", \"16\" },\n		correct = 3,\n		because = \"Four sides of four, so sixteen.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"its_apostrophe\",\n		ask = \"Which sentence is written correctly?\",\n		answers = { \"The dog wagged it's tail\", \"The dog wagged its tail\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Its shows belonging. It's is short for it is.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"skeleton\",\n		ask = \"What is your skeleton mostly there to do?\",\n		answers = { \"Hold you up\", \"Keep you warm\", \"Digest your food\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Your bones are the frame the rest of you is built on.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"average_three\",\n		ask = \"What is the average of 4, 6 and 8?\",\n		answers = { \"6\", \"7\", \"18\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"They add up to eighteen, and eighteen shared between three is six.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"largest_ocean\",\n		ask = \"Which is the largest ocean?\",\n		answers = { \"The Atlantic\", \"The Pacific\", \"The Arctic\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"The Pacific, and by a long way.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"prime_number\",\n		ask = \"Which of these is a prime number?\",\n		answers = { \"9\", \"15\", \"17\" },\n		correct = 3,\n		because = \"Seventeen. Nothing divides into it but one and itself.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"silent_letter\",\n		ask = \"Which letter is silent in the word knee?\",\n		answers = { \"The k\", \"The n\", \"The e\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"The k is written down but never said.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"century\",\n		ask = \"How many years are there in a century?\",\n		answers = { \"Ten\", \"One hundred\", \"One thousand\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"A hundred. A thousand years is a millennium.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"lungs\",\n		ask = \"What do your lungs do?\",\n		answers = { \"Take in air\", \"Pump blood\", \"Digest food\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"They take the oxygen out of the air you breathe in.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"rectangle_area\",\n		ask = \"A rectangle is 5 across and 3 down. What is its area?\",\n		answers = { \"8\", \"15\", \"16\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Multiply the two sides: five times three is fifteen.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"which_noun\",\n		ask = \"Which of these words is a noun?\",\n		answers = { \"Slowly\", \"Bridge\", \"Happily\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"A noun is a thing, and a bridge is a thing.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n\n	-- The last years, and the only ones that ask about the life the player is about to\n	-- be living. A sixteen-year-old in this game is a week away from a wage, so half of\n	-- these are about wages -- which is the closest school here gets to saying what it\n	-- is for.\n	{\n		id = \"percent_of\",\n		ask = \"What is 15% of 200?\",\n		answers = { \"15\", \"30\", \"45\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Ten percent is twenty and five percent is ten, so fifteen percent is thirty.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"solve_x\",\n		ask = \"If 3x = 21, what is x?\",\n		answers = { \"6\", \"7\", \"8\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Divide both sides by three and you are left with x = 7.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"supply_demand\",\n		ask = \"Lots of people want something and there is little of it. The price usually does what?\",\n		answers = { \"Goes up\", \"Goes down\", \"Stays the same\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Short supply and high demand push a price up.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"interest_year\",\n		ask = \"You save $100 at 5% a year. How much interest is that in a year?\",\n		answers = { \"$5\", \"$15\", \"$50\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Five percent of a hundred is five.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"plant_gas\",\n		ask = \"Which gas do plants take in to make their food?\",\n		answers = { \"Oxygen\", \"Carbon dioxide\", \"Nitrogen\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"They take in carbon dioxide and give out oxygen, which is the other half of breathing.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"metaphor\",\n		ask = \"The city never sleeps. What is that an example of?\",\n		answers = { \"A metaphor\", \"A statistic\", \"A rhyme\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"It gives a city a human habit, and that is what a metaphor does.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"deduction\",\n		ask = \"Money taken out of your pay before you get it is called what?\",\n		answers = { \"A deduction\", \"A bonus\", \"A refund\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"A deduction. Tax is the one almost everybody has.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"dna\",\n		ask = \"What does DNA mostly carry?\",\n		answers = { \"Instructions\", \"Blood\", \"Water\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"The instructions for building and running a living thing.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"moon_drop\",\n		ask = \"Drop a hammer and a feather on the Moon. What happens?\",\n		answers = { \"They land together\", \"The hammer lands first\", \"The feather lands first\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"There is no air up there to slow the feather, so gravity pulls both the same.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"deficit\",\n		ask = \"You earn $400 a month and spend $450. What is that called?\",\n		answers = { \"A surplus\", \"A deficit\", \"A profit\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"A deficit. Spending more than you take in leaves a hole.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"thesis\",\n		ask = \"What is the thesis of an essay?\",\n		answers = { \"The point it argues for\", \"Its last line\", \"Its list of sources\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"It is the claim the rest of the essay exists to back up.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"triangle_angles\",\n		ask = \"The angles inside a triangle add up to how many degrees?\",\n		answers = { \"90\", \"180\", \"360\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Always a hundred and eighty, whatever shape the triangle is.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"resume\",\n		ask = \"What is a resume for?\",\n		answers = { \"Showing an employer what you have done\", \"Paying your taxes\", \"Renting a home\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"It is the short account of your work an employer reads before they meet you.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"vote\",\n		ask = \"In a democracy, what does a vote decide?\",\n		answers = { \"Who holds office\", \"What the weather does\", \"What things cost\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Voting is how the people choose who governs them.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"water_made_of\",\n		ask = \"Water is made of hydrogen and what else?\",\n		answers = { \"Oxygen\", \"Carbon\", \"Helium\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Oxygen. H2O is two hydrogen and one oxygen.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"rent\",\n		ask = \"Money paid every month to live somewhere you do not own is called what?\",\n		answers = { \"Rent\", \"Wages\", \"A deposit\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Rent. A deposit is the one-off sum you leave at the start.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"speed\",\n		ask = \"You walk 12 miles in 3 hours. How fast were you going?\",\n		answers = { \"3 miles an hour\", \"4 miles an hour\", \"6 miles an hour\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Twelve miles shared over three hours is four an hour.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"late_payment\",\n		ask = \"You borrow money and pay it back late. What usually happens?\",\n		answers = { \"It costs you more\", \"It costs you less\", \"Nothing at all\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Interest keeps running and charges get added, so late borrowing is expensive borrowing.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"income_tax\",\n		ask = \"Income tax is worked out on what?\",\n		answers = { \"What you earn\", \"What you own\", \"Where you live\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"On your earnings. It is a share of what you are paid.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"negative_add\",\n		ask = \"What is -5 + 8?\",\n		answers = { \"3\", \"-3\", \"13\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Count up eight from minus five and you arrive at three.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"evaporation\",\n		ask = \"Wet clothes dry on a line. Where did the water go?\",\n		answers = { \"Into the air\", \"Into the ground\", \"Further into the cloth\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"It evaporated into the air, which is where puddles go too.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n\n	-- A4 additions: breadth across the three bands, with per-question teacher\n	-- reactions on the ones where the gesture matters more than the default bow.\n\n	-- Early (6-9/10): the questions a seven-year-old actually gets asked.\n	{\n		id = \"five_threes\",\n		ask = \"What is 5 groups of 3?\",\n		answers = { \"8\", \"15\", \"18\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Three, six, nine, twelve, fifteen — five threes make fifteen.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"coin_quarter\",\n		ask = \"Which coin is worth twenty-five cents?\",\n		answers = { \"Quarter\", \"Dime\", \"Nickel\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"A quarter. The name gives it away — a quarter of a dollar.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 10,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"season_after\",\n		ask = \"Which season comes right after winter?\",\n		answers = { \"Spring\", \"Summer\", \"Autumn\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Spring: the days get longer and things start growing again.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 8,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"rain_clouds\",\n		ask = \"Where does rain actually come from?\",\n		answers = { \"Clouds\", \"The ground\", \"The sun\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Clouds. Water evaporates, forms clouds, and falls back as rain.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"metre_longer\",\n		ask = \"Which is longer: a metre or a foot?\",\n		answers = { \"A metre\", \"A foot\", \"They are the same\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"A metre — just over three feet, which is why the metric system caught on.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 10,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"drawer_push\",\n		ask = \"A wooden drawer is stuck. What do you do?\",\n		answers = { \"Pull it\", \"Push it\", \"Kick it\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Pull it. Drawers open toward you, which is the whole point of a drawer.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 8,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"carrot_grow\",\n		ask = \"Where do carrots grow?\",\n		answers = { \"In the ground\", \"On trees\", \"In water\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Underground. The orange bit is the root, which is why you have to pull them up.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 9,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"clock_minutes\",\n		ask = \"How many minutes are in one hour?\",\n		answers = { \"60\", \"100\", \"24\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Sixty. The clock face has twelve fives, and twelve times five is sixty.\",\n		minAge = 6,\n		maxAge = 10,\n	},\n\n	-- Middle (9-13): the bridge between arithmetic and the world using it.\n	{\n		id = \"nine_squares\",\n		ask = \"What is 9 x 9?\",\n		answers = { \"72\", \"81\", \"89\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Eighty-one. Nine nines are eighty-one, which is also nine squared.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n		reaction = \"settle\",\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"quarter_twenty\",\n		ask = \"What is one quarter of 20?\",\n		answers = { \"4\", \"5\", \"10\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Five. A quarter means divide by four, and twenty divided by four is five.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n		reaction = \"settle\",\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"equal_half\",\n		ask = \"If two people split 1.50 equally, how much does each get?\",\n		answers = { \"75p\", \"50p\", \"1.00\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Seventy-five pence each — half of a pound fifty is seventy-five pence.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 12,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"theyre_their\",\n		ask = \"Which is correct: 'they are happy' or 'their happy'?\",\n		answers = { \"They are happy\", \"Their happy\", \"Neither\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"'They are' contracts to 'they're'. 'Their' belongs to somebody.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"seconds_minute\",\n		ask = \"How many seconds are in three minutes?\",\n		answers = { \"120\", \"180\", \"300\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"One hundred and eighty — three times sixty.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"renewable\",\n		ask = \"Which of these is a renewable energy source?\",\n		answers = { \"Coal\", \"Solar\", \"Natural gas\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Solar. The sun is not running out any time soon; fossil fuels are.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 13,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"map_north\",\n		ask = \"On a map, which direction is typically at the top?\",\n		answers = { \"North\", \"South\", \"East\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"North. Maps are conventionally drawn with north at the top.\",\n		minAge = 9,\n		maxAge = 12,\n	},\n\n	-- Late (13-17): the questions that use the world they are about to enter.\n	{\n		id = \"extra_hours\",\n		ask = \"You work 2 extra hours at $10 an hour. How much do you earn?\",\n		answers = { \"$20\", \"$12\", \"$200\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Twenty dollars. Two times ten, which is the arithmetic every payslip uses.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"net_pay\",\n		ask = \"Your payslip says $500 gross and $400 net. What is the difference?\",\n		answers = { \"Tax and deductions\", \"A mistake\", \"Your bonus\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Net is what you take home after tax and other deductions come out of the gross.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n		reaction = \"settle\",\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"quarter_rent\",\n		ask = \"Rent is $800 a month. What do you pay in a quarter?\",\n		answers = { \"$200\", \"$2,400\", \"$400\" },\n		correct = 2,\n		because = \"Two thousand four hundred — three months at eight hundred each.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"fixed_cost\",\n		ask = \"Which of these is a fixed cost?\",\n		answers = { \"Monthly rent\", \"Electricity bill\", \"Groceries\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Rent stays the same each month. The others change with how much you use.\",\n		minAge = 13,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"inflation\",\n		ask = \"Prices rise 3% a year. What happens to your savings?\",\n		answers = {\n			\"They buy less over time\",\n			\"They buy more over time\",\n			\"Nothing changes\",\n		},\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Inflation erodes purchasing power — the same money buys fewer things each year.\",\n		minAge = 14,\n		maxAge = 17,\n		reaction = \"approach\",\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"compound\",\n		ask = \"You earn 10% interest, then 10% on that. Is the total more than 20%?\",\n		answers = { \"Yes\", \"No\", \"It depends on the bank\" },\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"Yes — compound interest earns interest on interest, so it grows faster than simple addition.\",\n		minAge = 14,\n		maxAge = 17,\n		reaction = \"approach\",\n	},\n	{\n		id = \"contract\",\n		ask = \"You sign a contract. What does it create?\",\n		answers = {\n			\"A legal obligation\",\n			\"A friendly promise\",\n			\"An optional agreement\",\n		},\n		correct = 1,\n		because = \"A contract is a legally binding agreement — breaking it has consequences a handshake does not.\",\n		minAge = 14,\n		maxAge = 17,\n	},\n}\n\n-- Content mistakes are authoring bugs, so these throw at start rather than producing a\n-- lesson that quietly has a question with two right answers in it. Same reasoning as\n-- JobTasks: the symptom of a bad question is not an error, it is a player being marked\n-- wrong for being right, and nobody would think to look in a content file for that.\nlocal function validate(question: LessonQuestion, seen: { [string]: true })\n	local id = question.id\n\n	if seen[id] then\n		error(`[AGES content] two lesson questions are called \"{id}\"`)\n	end\n	seen[id] = true\n\n	if question.ask == \"\" then\n		error(`[AGES content] question \"{id}\" asks nothing`)\n	end\n	if #question.ask > MAX_ASK then\n		error(\n			`[AGES content] question \"{id}\" is {#question.ask} characters, over the {MAX_ASK} a player can read `\n				.. `off a HUD row while walking`\n		)\n	end\n	if question.because == \"\" then\n		error(\n			`[AGES content] question \"{id}\" has no explanation; it is the only thing that makes getting this `\n				.. `wrong worth anything`\n		)\n	end\n\n	local answers = question.answers\n	if #answers < MIN_ANSWERS or #answers > MAX_ANSWERS then\n		error(\n			`[AGES content] question \"{id}\" offers {#answers} answers; it must be from {MIN_ANSWERS} to {MAX_ANSWERS}, `\n				.. `because that is how many pads fit on the floor`\n		)\n	end\n\n	-- Two answers reading the same is the one authoring mistake that cannot be seen by\n	-- reading the lesson: the player walks onto a pad saying exactly what they were\n	-- asked for and is marked wrong, because it was the other one.\n	local written: { [string]: true } = {}\n	for index, answer in answers do\n		if answer == \"\" then\n			error(`[AGES content] question \"{id}\" has an answer with nothing written on it`)\n		end\n		if written[answer] then\n			error(`[AGES content] question \"{id}\" offers \"{answer}\" twice; only one of them can be the right one`)\n		end\n		written[answer] = true\n		if #answer > Config.Enact.MaxLabelLength then\n			error(\n				`[AGES content] answer {index} of \"{id}\" is {#answer} characters, over the `\n					.. `{Config.Enact.MaxLabelLength} that fits a panel row without wrapping to three lines`\n			)\n		end\n	end\n\n	if question.correct ~= math.floor(question.correct) or question.correct < 1 or question.correct > #answers then\n		error(`[AGES content] question \"{id}\" says answer {question.correct} is right, and there is no such answer`)\n	end\n\n	local school = Config.School\n	if question.minAge > question.maxAge then\n		error(`[AGES content] question \"{id}\" is asked from {question.minAge} to {question.maxAge}, which is no ages`)\n	end\n	if question.minAge < school.StartAge or question.maxAge > school.EndAge then\n		error(\n			`[AGES content] question \"{id}\" is asked from {question.minAge} to {question.maxAge}, outside the `\n				.. `{school.StartAge}-{school.EndAge} school runs over; it would never be asked`\n		)\n	end\nend\n\nlocal seen: { [string]: true } = {}\nfor _, question in SOURCE do\n	validate(question, seen)\nend\n\n-- The check that actually matters, and the reason the age windows overlap.\n--\n-- A lesson asks QuestionsPerLesson distinct questions, so an age with fewer than that\n-- in the bank silently runs a short lesson -- which grades out of the wrong total and\n-- looks, from the outside, like the lesson broke halfway through. Caught here because\n-- the alternative is discovering it as an eleven-year-old, once, on somebody's save.\nfor age = Config.School.StartAge, Config.School.EndAge do\n	local available = 0\n	for _, question in SOURCE do\n		if age >= question.minAge and age <= question.maxAge then\n			available += 1\n		end\n	end\n	if available < Config.School.QuestionsPerLesson then\n		error(\n			`[AGES content] age {age} has only {available} questions written for it and a lesson asks `\n				.. `{Config.School.QuestionsPerLesson}; widen an age window or write more`\n		)\n	end\nend\n\n-- The questions a lesson at this age is made of, in the order they are asked.\n--\n-- Distinct within the lesson and shuffled across it, which are two different needs:\n-- distinct because being asked the same thing twice in five minutes is the game\n-- forgetting what it just said, and shuffled because the bank is written in age order\n-- and a lesson that walked it top-down would ask the easiest five every time.\n--\n-- Returns fewer than asked for only if the bank is thin, which the check above makes\n-- impossible at start -- so the caller can treat a short list as a real lesson rather\n-- than as a failure, and grades it out of what it actually got.\nfunction Lessons.For(age: number, count: number): { LessonQuestion }\n	local pool: { LessonQuestion } = {}\n	for _, question in SOURCE do\n		if age >= question.minAge and age <= question.maxAge then\n			table.insert(pool, question)\n		end\n	end\n\n	for index = #pool, 2, -1 do\n		local swap = math.random(index)\n		pool[index], pool[swap] = pool[swap], pool[index]\n	end\n\n	local picked: { LessonQuestion } = {}\n	for index = 1, math.min(count, #pool) do\n		table.insert(picked, pool[index])\n	end\n	return picked\nend\n\nreturn Lessons\n"
